<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas the Apostle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Saint Thomas, Doubting Thomas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas the Apostle was one of the twelve apostles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, remembered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a> for doubting the resurrection until he saw Jesus. Tradition holds that he preached in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and was martyred near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-thomas-christians">Saint Thomas Christians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> trace their origin to him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-thomas">Acts of Thomas</a> links him with the Indo-Parthian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondophares">Gondophares</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-the-apostle.jpg" alt="Thomas the Apostle" width="320" height="283" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mortel · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_the_Apostle._Detail_of_the_mosaic_in_the_Basilica_of_San_Vitale._Ravena,_Italy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
